message.content:
You are an archaeology worker for the Fortress revival. Load the `fortress-repo` skill first.

**Reader and question.** This closes the curator's process-engineering study (`explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/README.md`). The question: did the rewrite of the `fortress-repo` skill change the quality of the batch workers' work, or its token efficiency? The rewrite landed on 2026-10-08, together with the batch workflow's redesign (`process-engineering/batch-redesign.md`), just before climb batch 11. Compare batches 8, 9 and 10 with batches 11, 12 and 13. Use exactly the methodology of the earlier studies. The curator's word: their scripts are recovered from the transcripts, not reinvented.

**The earlier studies, read these first:**
- `process-engineering/labor.md` (batches 8 and 9, tokens by role and by kind of work);
- `testing-practices.md` (batches 8 to 10, re-learning how to build and run tests, the eight practices);
- `context-study.md` (batch 10's rung workers before their first edit, rediscovery of facts on record);
- `checking-roles-cost.md` (batch 10's checking roles by labor.md's method);
- `harness-cache-cost.md` (test runs starting with an empty cache).

Each says its scripts were kept in a scratch folder and not committed. Those folders are gone.

**Step 1, recover the scripts.** The workers that wrote those notes were `Agent`-tool workers of session `fe616d40`. Their transcripts are at:
- `/root/.claude/projects/-home-user-fortress/fe616d40-a9c6-56d7-9da1-7168a172765d/subagents/agent-*.jsonl`;
- the backup copy under `/home/user/fortress-transcripts-blinded/projects/-home-user-fortress/fe616d40-a9c6-56d7-9da1-7168a172765d/`, which also holds files missing from the live folder.

Find each note's author by searching the transcripts with bounded greps for the note's path in a write or a commit. From that author's tool calls, recover the final text of every script it used: files it created with Write or with a shell heredoc, its later edits, and inline `python3 - <<EOF` programs. Commit them under `explorations/coordinator/tools/transcript-study/`. Add a `README.md` that gives, for each script, the note it serves, the transcript and the call it was recovered from, and how to run it. Fix a path or a run id only where the original pointed into its own scratch. Say every such change in the README.

**Step 2, show it is the same method.** Re-run the recovered scripts on the batches each note measured. Reproduce the notes' headline figures, for example:
- labor.md's 14.60M written over 43 agents and its shares;
- testing-practices.md's about 3M of 119.5M;
- context-study.md's rediscovery figures;
- checking-roles-cost.md's refusal chains.

Explain every difference.

**Step 3, run them on batches 11 to 13.** The runs are `wf_88561d30-63b` (11), `wf_7814a5bc-549` (12) and `wf_33c4de68-284` (13), beside `wf_603242ca-111` (8), `wf_f747fd3e-9e4` (9) and `wf_d5ec6194-bcc` (10), under `.../subagents/workflows/<run>/` in either location. `explorations/coordinator/tools/batch-measures.py` and `journal-text.py` read them. Add one measure the old studies lacked, written as a committed script in the same folder: per agent, the calls that load the `fortress-repo` skill and the parts of `.claude/skills/` it reads.

**Step 4, quality side by side.** From each batch's review (`explorations/reviews/batch-8-review.md` to `batch-13-review.md`, Part 2 and the conformance part), tabulate:
- skeptic fixes by kind;
- defects found after the rung;
- refusal cycles and rulings;
- test-first compliance;
- misses of the record or of the library's own way.

Read only. Do not re-judge.

**Step 5, the note and the closure.**
- **The note.** Write `explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/skill-effect.md`: the before-and-after by mechanism, and what moved and what did not, with its numbers. Name the confounds:
  - the redesign landed at the same time;
  - the batches' work differs;
  - the restarts in batches 9 and 13 re-ran agents;
  - batch 13's rung N built two rungs' work.
  Say what can be attributed to the skill and what cannot.
- **The README.** Close `process-engineering/README.md`:
  - what is done;
  - each item of "Pending, in order" settled, dropped or still open, with why;
  - "Waiting on Pavol" brought up to date;
  - the recovered tools named as the study's reusable process, for each later post-batch review to run.
- **INDEX lines.** Add one to `explorations/coordinator/INDEX.md` for the new note and for the tool folder's README.

**Rules.**
- Read only, except the new tool folder, the note, the study README and the INDEX lines.
- Scratch goes under `/home/user/fortress/tmp/skill-effect/`.
- Read transcripts through scripts and bounded output only, never whole.
- Commit only your own paths: `git add -- <paths> && git commit -m "…" -- <paths>`, with this footer exactly:
  ```
  Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
  Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AmiXNpJxQ6TBwec4vJZHDB
  ```
  Do not push.
- Count tokens as writes only: input plus cache creation, one count per message id.
- A command longer than 270 seconds goes to a background script that you poll.
- Other workers are writing in this tree under `explorations/perf-probes/`. Do not touch those files.
- Return at most 20 lines:
  - which scripts you recovered, and whether they reproduced the old figures;
  - the headline before and after;
  - what can and cannot be attributed to the skill;
  - the study's items as closed.
